            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Power of the Command Line</h2>
                    <p>The .NET CLI (Command Line Interface) is a cross-platform tool for developing, building, running, and publishing .NET applications. It's the foundation of .NET development—even IDEs like Visual Studio use the CLI under the hood.</p>
                    
                    <p>Mastering the CLI will make you more productive and give you a deeper understanding of how .NET works.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Basic CLI Structure</h2>
                    <p>All .NET CLI commands follow this pattern:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet [command] [arguments] [options]</code></pre>

                    <p>For example:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Create a new console application
dotnet new console --name MyApp

# Build the current project
dotnet build

# Run the application
dotnet run</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Creating Projects with 'dotnet new'</h2>
                    <p>The <code>dotnet new</code> command creates new projects and files from templates:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># See all available templates
dotnet new list

# Create a console application
dotnet new console -n MyConsoleApp

# Create a web API
dotnet new webapi -n MyApi

# Create a Blazor WebAssembly app
dotnet new blazorwasm -n MyBlazorApp

# Create a class library
dotnet new classlib -n MyLibrary

# Create a solution file
dotnet new sln -n MySolution</code></pre>

                    <h3>Common Templates</h3>
                    <div class="template-grid">
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>console</code>
                            <span>Console Application</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>webapi</code>
                            <span>ASP.NET Core Web API</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>mvc</code>
                            <span>ASP.NET Core MVC</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>blazorwasm</code>
                            <span>Blazor WebAssembly</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>blazorserver</code>
                            <span>Blazor Server</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>classlib</code>
                            <span>Class Library</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>xunit</code>
                            <span>xUnit Test Project</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="template-card">
                            <code>worker</code>
                            <span>Worker Service</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Building Projects</h2>
                    <p>The <code>dotnet build</code> command compiles your project:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Build the project in the current directory
dotnet build

# Build in Release mode
dotnet build -c Release

# Build a specific project
dotnet build MyProject.csproj

# Build with verbose output
dotnet build -v detailed</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Build Configurations</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Debug</strong> (default): Includes debugging symbols, no optimizations. Use for development.<br/>
                        <strong>Release</strong>: Optimized code, smaller binaries. Use for production.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Running Applications</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>dotnet run</code> to build and execute your application:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Build and run the current project
dotnet run

# Run in Release mode
dotnet run -c Release

# Pass arguments to your application
dotnet run -- arg1 arg2

# Run a specific project
dotnet run --project ./src/MyApp/MyApp.csproj

# Run without building (use existing build)
dotnet run --no-build</code></pre>

                    <h3>Hot Reload with 'dotnet watch'</h3>
                    <p>During development, use <code>dotnet watch</code> to automatically restart your app when files change:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Run with hot reload
dotnet watch run

# Run tests with watch
dotnet watch test</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Managing Dependencies</h2>
                    <p>Add, remove, and list NuGet package references:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Add a NuGet package
dotnet add package Newtonsoft.Json

# Add a specific version
dotnet add package Serilog --version 3.0.0

# Remove a package
dotnet remove package Newtonsoft.Json

# List all packages
dotnet list package

# Check for outdated packages
dotnet list package --outdated</code></pre>

                    <h3>Project References</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Add a reference to another project
dotnet add reference ../MyLibrary/MyLibrary.csproj

# Remove a project reference
dotnet remove reference ../MyLibrary/MyLibrary.csproj

# List project references
dotnet list reference</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Working with Solutions</h2>
                    <p>Solutions group multiple projects together:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Create a new solution
dotnet new sln -n MySolution

# Add projects to the solution
dotnet sln add ./src/MyApp/MyApp.csproj
dotnet sln add ./src/MyLibrary/MyLibrary.csproj
dotnet sln add ./tests/MyApp.Tests/MyApp.Tests.csproj

# List projects in the solution
dotnet sln list

# Remove a project from the solution
dotnet sln remove ./src/OldProject/OldProject.csproj

# Build the entire solution
dotnet build MySolution.sln</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Running Tests</h2>
                    <p>Execute unit tests with the <code>dotnet test</code> command:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Run all tests
dotnet test

# Run with detailed output
dotnet test -v normal

# Run specific tests by name
dotnet test --filter "ClassName=MyTests"

# Generate code coverage
dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage"

# Run tests in parallel
dotnet test --parallel</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Publishing Applications</h2>
                    <p>The <code>dotnet publish</code> command prepares your app for deployment:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Publish for the current platform
dotnet publish -c Release

# Publish as self-contained (includes runtime)
dotnet publish -c Release --self-contained

# Publish for a specific platform
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64
dotnet publish -c Release -r linux-x64
dotnet publish -c Release -r osx-arm64

# Publish as single file
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained -p:PublishSingleFile=true</code></pre>

